import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BN3Eol-0.js";const p=JSON.parse(`{"title":"UXD-049 — A deep-sky object's panel has an IN THE SKY tab","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-049-deep-sky-panel-in-the-sky-tab.md","filePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-049-deep-sky-panel-in-the-sky-tab.md"}`),n={name:"uxs/decisions/UXD-049-deep-sky-panel-in-the-sky-tab.md"};function r(i,e,d,l,c,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="uxd-049-—-a-deep-sky-object-s-panel-has-an-in-the-sky-tab" tabindex="-1">UXD-049 — A deep-sky object&#39;s panel has an IN THE SKY tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#uxd-049-—-a-deep-sky-object-s-panel-has-an-in-the-sky-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;UXD-049 — A deep-sky object&#39;s panel has an IN THE SKY tab&quot;">​</a></h1><ul><li><strong>Status</strong>: accepted (built; unit tests green, e2e and screenshots in Verification)</li><li><strong>Date</strong>: 2026-11-15</li><li><strong>Decider</strong>: the operator, 2026-11-15 — all six recommendations of the L42 brief approved (L42-1a, L42-2a, L42-3a, L42-4a, L42-5a, L42-6a)</li><li><strong>Answers</strong>: L42 (B3 brief Q6)</li><li><strong>Builds on</strong>: <a href="./UXD-008-star-panel-one-hero-and-in-the-sky-tab">UXD-008</a> and <a href="./UXD-023-star-and-outer-panels-follow-the-planet-pattern">UXD-023</a> (the star&#39;s IN THE SKY tab and the shared panel body); brief <code>docs/wip/2026-11-15-L42-constellation-finder-brief.md</code> §13</li><li><strong>Changes</strong>: <code>src/lib/universe/sky-finder.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/universe/constellation-actions.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/universe/constellation-finder.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/components/ConstellationFinder.svelte</code>, <code>DeepSkyPanel.svelte</code>, <code>StarPanel.svelte</code> (the Serpens lookup and the shared action), 13 message keys in 14 locales; amended 2026-11-15: <code>static/data/universe/sun-ephemeris.json</code>, <code>scripts/refresh-sun-ephemeris.mjs</code>, <code>scripts/check-sky-rows-ephemeris.ts</code>, data-sources entry <code>jpl-horizons-sun</code> (description in 14 locales)</li></ul><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On <code>?deepsky=M42</code> the reader learned &quot;Constellation: Orion&quot; and could not see where in Orion, or how to find it. A star&#39;s panel already has the constellation finder (UXD-008); B3 left deep-sky objects out on purpose (brief Q6), not for a technical reason: all 143 objects carry a constellation code, an RA and Dec, and all 47 codes in use have a line figure.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>A deep-sky panel&#39;s tabs are OVERVIEW · IN THE SKY</strong> (L42-1a, L42-2a), the same second slot as the star panel. The tab is always there; the constellation cell stays on OVERVIEW as well.</li><li><strong>The finder draws the object as a ring</strong> (<code>ConstellationFinder</code> <code>marker=&quot;object&quot;</code>; the star glow is unchanged for stars), with the nearest named star as a labelled dot and a dashed hairline to the object when that star falls inside the box the figure already fills (it never shrinks the figure).</li><li><strong>Six cells, all computed from the object&#39;s own RA / Dec / magnitude</strong> (<code>sky-finder.ts</code>; no new sourced claim): <ul><li>CONSTELLATION (accent) and POSITION (<code>5h 35m · −5°</code>, numbers only).</li><li>NEAREST BRIGHT STAR: the nearest of the 62 named stars, only within 15 degrees (L42-3a); the row is left out beyond (39 of 143 objects), never a far star. The star&#39;s name is its localized name when its overlay has one.</li><li>BEST MONTHS (amended 2026-11-15, operator: the first rule, a mean-Sun calendar rule, was not compared with a planetarium; replaced by an ephemeris): the Sun&#39;s apparent RA / Dec for every day of 2026 come from <strong>NASA JPL Horizons</strong> (<code>static/data/universe/sun-ephemeris.json</code>, fetched by <code>scripts/refresh-sun-ephemeris.mjs</code>, query, URL and date in the file; credited as data-sources entry <code>jpl-horizons-sun</code>, listed in the /library Planetary models block and on /credits). <strong>Amended 2026-11-19 (operator, search backlog P1):</strong> a month is best when the object stands opposite the Sun, so it is highest around midnight: the Sun&#39;s RA on the 15th of the month within <strong>3 hours (45 degrees)</strong> of the object&#39;s RA + 180 degrees (the object&#39;s J2000 position precessed to mid-2026, up to 0.4 degree). That is the observing guides&#39; convention and gives a run of three months (<code>Nov to Jan</code>; a window can cross the year end). The row is shown only where the object climbs at least <strong>20 degrees</strong> from the reader&#39;s latitude; <strong>never that high, or an unknown time zone: the row is left out</strong>. The rule it replaces (hours at least 20 degrees up while the Sun is below −18 degrees, 4 hours a night) put summer objects in winter from 45 N up, where early-summer nights have no astronomical darkness: M13 read &quot;Feb to Apr&quot; at 52 N, observers use June and July. The row carries a &quot;JPL Horizons&quot; link. Month names from <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> in the reader&#39;s locale.</li><li>FROM YOUR LATITUDE: the highest altitude is <code>90 − |latitude − dec of date|</code> plus Saemundsson refraction (1010 hPa, 10 C; about 0.02 degree at 38); over 60 degrees reads &quot;High in the sky&quot;, under 20 &quot;Low on the horizon&quot;, 0 or less &quot;Never rises&quot;, the middle says nothing. The latitude is the browser time zone&#39;s (<code>viewerLatLon</code>, no permission prompt, L42-6a); the row is left out when the zone is unknown.</li><li>SEEN WITH: a magnitude rule (L42-4a): to magnitude 6 naked eye, to 9 binoculars, above that a telescope; <strong>no magnitude, no row</strong> (20 of 143). It is a rule on the catalogue V magnitude, not a promise: a large faint object (M31, M33, M44, M42) is listed by its summed magnitude and is easy only under a dark sky.</li></ul></li><li><strong>The zodiac note and the &quot;Show constellation lines&quot; action</strong> are the star tab&#39;s (same keys, one shared <code>showConstellationLines</code>).</li><li><strong>The frame is never guessed.</strong> The figures and the named stars are in the HYG frame (z toward the north pole); a deep-sky object&#39;s <code>x, y, z</code> is the scene frame (y north), <code>(x, z, y)</code> of it. The finder is fed <code>raDecToHyg(ra, dec)</code> only; a test compares it with all 143 objects&#39; <code>x, y, z</code>.</li><li><strong>Serpens&#39; two-part figure merges</strong> (<code>figureVertices</code> returns every entry of a code), so M5, M16 and the Serpens Nebula see both halves; <code>StarPanel</code>, which took the first entry only, uses the same helper.</li><li><strong>The entity pages are not touched</strong> (L42-5a); the latitude row would be client-only there. Plan row L61.</li><li><strong>Amended 2026-11-18 (plan D1 / L61): the entity pages carry the section.</strong> The tab body is one component per kind, <code>DeepSkyInTheSky.svelte</code> and <code>StarInTheSky.svelte</code>, rendered by the panels&#39; IN THE SKY tab and by <code>/explore/deep-sky/&lt;id&gt;</code> and <code>/explore/stars/&lt;id&gt;</code> as an &quot;IN THE SKY&quot; section after the facts (named stars only: a host-only star has no sky position). On the prerendered page the latitude rows (BEST MONTHS, FROM YOUR LATITUDE) are read after mount, from the reader&#39;s browser, never from the build machine. The lines action is a link there: it turns the lines on and opens the object in the explorer (<code>?deepsky=&lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>?goto=&lt;id&gt;</code>). The finder is held to 28 rem on the reading page. e2e: <code>tests/e2e/entity-in-the-sky.spec.ts</code>.</li></ol><h2 id="not-done-here" tabindex="-1">Not done here <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-done-here" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not done here&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><s>The entity pages draw no finder</s> (done 2026-11-18, item 8).</li><li>At panel width the three-column facts grid breaks long labels mid-word (&quot;CONSTELLAT ION&quot;, &quot;NEAREST BRIGHT STAR&quot;; <code>UniverseFacts</code> <code>overflow-wrap: anywhere</code>), in every universe panel, not only this tab: raised with the operator 2026-11-18.</li><li>20 objects have no catalogue magnitude, so no SEEN WITH row (M35 and the Carina Nebula are visible to the eye in reality; filling <code>mag</code> is a catalogue task).</li><li>The nearest-star list is the 62 curated stars, not a bright-star catalogue.</li><li>Constellation names are English in all 14 locales (B3 finding 6), unchanged.</li><li>No planetarium program was run. Month windows and altitudes are compared with JPL Horizons observer tables (the Sun&#39;s topocentric elevation and sidereal time every 15 minutes, 30 objects at 52.5 N, 20 N, 34 S and 52.5 N 180 E) and USNO&#39;s celestial-navigation service (altitude and position of date of 349 star sightings): <code>docs/wip/2026-11-15-sky-rows-ephemeris-check.md</code>, pinned by <code>sky-finder.test.ts</code>. Horizons has no fixed-RA / Dec target and USNO lists only the Sun, Moon, planets and navigational stars, so a deep-sky object itself is never looked up in a tool.</li><li>The 20 degrees, the 18 degrees and the 4 hours are our thresholds, not a standard; moonlight, light pollution and horizon obstructions are not modelled.</li></ul><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Unit: <code>src/lib/universe/sky-finder.test.ts</code> (frame against all 143 objects, hop distances, months, altitude at several latitudes, band edges, Serpens merge, counts: 104 objects with a hop, 29 / 61 / 33 / 20 bands), <code>constellation-finder.test.ts</code> (the hop point never changes the figure).</li><li>e2e: <code>tests/e2e/explore-deep-sky-panel.spec.ts</code> (M42, M31, M44, cas-a, M5, 30 Doradus with an unknown time zone).</li><li>Screenshots at 1440 and 390 for M42, M31, M5, 30 Doradus, cas-a, M42 in <code>de</code> and <code>ar</code>.</li></ul>',10)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
